From 6fb99adb593094cf2728e60b64896bdbfaa72e07 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Osamah <omfs249@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 07:16:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Make a picture you add actually appear on the bowl
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Adding an image put the object in the layer, showed it in the properties
panel and baked it correctly — and drew nothing on the canvas. The report
was "it disappears, even though it works when I bake it", which is exactly
what the code did.

Texture.from(resource) is a cache keyed on the resource object's identity
(pixi's textureFrom.js ends with Cache.set(resource, texture)), so the ghost
that follows the cursor and the object you drop — one ImageBitmap — were
handed the same Texture. Placing the object destroys the ghost, and
destroy({ textureSource: true }) then tore the GPU source out from under the
object that had just been created: an empty canvas and "Cannot read
properties of null (reading 'alphaMode')". Text and shapes were never hit,
because renderObjectCanvas builds them a fresh canvas each call, which is a
fresh cache key by accident. render/ownTexture.ts is now the only way either
one gets a texture, and it skips the cache.

The rest of the flow, on every device:

- A phone camera hands you 4000-8000px on the long edge. That is a 47MB GPU
  texture, and past 4096 it is larger than many mobile GPUs will accept at
  all — the picture is simply absent, with nothing reported. Imports now
  decode to 2048 on the long edge: 12MB, and free, because the widest an
  import can be placed is 1250 seats = 1333 cells, which 2048 feeds with
  room to spare. verify checks that pairing so it cannot drift.
- `body.m-shell .tool-bar { display:none }` hid the desktop import row, so a
  phone user who picked a photo saw nothing happen: no width, no stand, no
  Place, no Cancel, no way out of import mode. The toolbar now announces a
  decoded file and the shell brings the options up in a sheet; dismissing
  that sheet cancels the import instead of leaving it armed and invisible.
- Place used to switch off in "click on canvas" mode. Touch has no hover, so
  there is no ghost to aim with and that was the one button that could have
  worked. It now drops the picture in the middle of the current view.
- A placed object gets Bake / Options / Delete floating above the ribbon.
  All three lived in the Paint sheet, below the brush controls, off the
  bottom of a scrolling panel.
- Picking Text or Shapes on a phone CLOSED the sheet — showToolBar built the
  body and then called open('add'), which returns every borrowed section and
  toggles. Sub-views now open properly, and the tab you came in through
  takes you back to its grid rather than closing.
- Every sentence in the flow is translated. The Arabic editor was answering
  `"images.png" added: drag to position, resize from the corner, then Bake`.

Measured, not asserted: scripts/image-ux.mts drives the real editor, decodes
a screenshot and counts the test picture's own colours, so the claim is "the
user can see it" and not "it is in the object layer". 47 checks across
desktop, Arabic RTL and a 390x844 phone. A drag costs 0.03ms of JS per move
and placement blocks for ~20ms, so the lag was the broken texture, not the
panel — the rAF throttling I had planned would have bought nothing.

